course: dictionary
lesson: 4851
title: Hyperbole: the big exaggeration
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Hyperbole is a huge exaggeration. The speaker does not mean it literally, so do not take it word for word. Writers and speakers use it to make a point, to make people laugh, or to show strong feeling. «I've told you a million times» does not mean you have been counted to a million.
explain: Hyperbole is everywhere in everyday talk. «I'm starving» usually means you are very hungry, not that you are about to faint. «This bag weighs a ton» means it is very heavy. Ask yourself: could this be true in real life? If not, it is probably hyperbole.
explain: Be careful with adverts and politics. Hyperbole can make a product or an idea sound better than it really is, so a good reader checks the facts before believing a big claim.
fact: Hyperbole means a huge exaggeration that is not meant to be taken literally.
fact: Hyperbole is a figure of speech, like simile and metaphor.
fact: «I've told you a million times» is hyperbole: the number is not meant to be counted.
fact: Hyperbole can be used for humour, drama or strong feeling.
fact: Hyperbole makes things bigger, understatement makes them smaller, and sarcasm says the opposite of what is meant.
exercise: Ruby says, «I've practised this a million times.» Does she really mean a million? | *No, it is hyperbole, a huge exaggeration to show she has practised a lot | Yes, she is only guessing the number of times | Yes, she has counted every single time
exercise: Grandad says, «We'll be here till we're ninety.» What is he probably doing? | Giving his exact age | *Making a big exaggeration about waiting a long time | Counting the minutes | Describing the weather
exercise: Which of these is hyperbole? | *I'm starving | The jacket is blue | It is three o'clock | The bus left at 8:15
test: Cerys says, «The thunder's a million miles off.» Should she count the seconds to see how far away it is? | No, because she should just guess | No, because a million miles is a real distance | Yes, because the words are always true | *Yes, because "a million miles" is hyperbole and the real clue is the time between lightning and thunder
test: «Bit of a wait, eh» makes something sound smaller than it is. What is this called? | Hyperbole | Simile | Sarcasm | *Understatement
exam: An advert says, «The best toaster in the whole world, it never burns anything ever!» What should a careful reader do? | Count every word in the advert | Ignore the whole advert because it has a joke in it | Believe it straight away, because the words are big | *Check the facts, because hyperbole can make a product sound better than it really is
